{"kind":"report","text":"Decision for7c52d274 under3957/1311: adopt the proposed supplemental finite Git object-closure check before final layout freeze. A successful extension must not knowingly attach the new commit to a prior Git graph whose named reachable objects are missing or corrupt. This clarifies9f5ff41d's “explicitly named parent object closure”; it does not replace that report's immediate-parent semantic checks.\n\n1. Keep the immediate prior commit's full scope/incarnation/format, strictly earlier native cutoff, canonical manifest/head, represented native prefix and retained/segment preservation checks. In addition, verify the complete Git object graph reachable from that supplied prior commit: each reachable commit, its tree, recursively named subtrees/blobs, and its parent through a genuinely parentless archive commit. Validate selected-format IDs and actual type/content hash; retain the supported fixed archive commit grammar and strict plain archive tree modes. No success at a caller-selected stop ID, missing older ancestor, unsupported shape or exhausted allowance. A parentless initial build has no older Git closure to read.\n\n2. This is a finite, budgeted traversal, not an unlimited ancestry promise. Use the SAME original ArchiveBudget, deadline and cumulative ledgers plus the published downward-only JournalWork limits. Reserve reads, body copying/hash/parse and traversal bookkeeping before doing them. Cached or already-seen objects may avoid another physical read, but edge lookup/validation and cache work still consume their published allowance; use bounded visited state, not an uncharged cache path or repeated subtree expansion. Stop the whole build on missing/corrupt/limit/late work, and leave no accessible successful partial output. The two checkpoint findings in084582e9 apply here too: freeze validated primitive probe metadata before an await and route all work-limit failures through terminal state. Explicitly document that many prior archive commits may make an otherwise small current prefix unavailable under the initial limits. Do not raise/reset those limits to make a fixture pass.\n\n3. Keep the distinction between mechanical Git reachability and native semantic/replay proof clear. This supplemental walk verifies older Git commit/tree/blob integrity and closure only; it does not reconstruct every historical cutoff's native semantics or redo every historical manifest-to-prefix comparison. Do not claim that it does. The existing immediate-parent semantic checks remain mandatory. It never follows commit hashes embedded inside retained native ancestry snapshots or foreign histories: those are blob payload, and their native ancestry status stays incomplete as already specified. Only actual Git commit/tree edges authorize traversal; no retained-store enumeration, later native head or unrelated object scan.\n\n4. The result is ephemeral local validation for this internal builder call. It is neither a persisted verified-closure certificate nor an at-send same-repository retention guarantee, and cannot be substituted for either. G1/108e7b79, provider admission/export, clone replay/receipt integrity, privacy/consent/disclosure and the full archive outcomes remain owed. A source whose objects can later disappear has not thereby acquired a retention lease. No legacy Reader/provider behavior or new external caller is authorized by this decision.\n\nRequired added witnesses: in each object format, a valid multi-commit prior graph extends and real local Git fsck/read-back succeeds; removing only its older grandparent while keeping the immediate parent's represented tree intact refuses; wrong older type/hash and missing older tree/blob refuse; downward object/work/deadline limits stop before unbounded traversal or partial output, including shared subtrees/cache hits and a late read. Preserve the immediate-parent content counterexample. A negative fixture with missing objects is intentionally invalid Git; distinguish it from the complete positive fixture. Add the explicit mechanical-versus-native qualification and finite availability limit to the implementation preparation/Source record. Then perform focused checks and one frozen gate before complete Source review.\n\nBasis checked: full7c52d274 request, full adopted9f5ff41d decision, durability15.4 deterministic parent/layout and15.5 rebuild/resolve/fairness passages, plus actual ArchiveBudget's terminal cumulative spend/original-deadline implementation. This is a pre-Source decision only. No Source edit, test/gate, push, deployment, provider operation, certificate or implementation approval was performed to answer this request.","body":{"formal_approval":"false","no_git_artifact":"true","promise":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:c4a06ae25be806409100037635525d008583d41f","request":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:7c52d2742ae473fb6819269ca662ebe35ff98c90"}}